sh/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Botok","dish/botok"],["Brem","dish/brem"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur-ketan-hitam"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-food"],["Cendol","dish/cendol"],["Cenil","dish/cenil"],["Cilok","dish/cilok"],["Cimpa","dish/cimpa"],["Corn fritter","dish/corn-fritter"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Dadiah","dish/dadiah"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Emping","dish/emping"],["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Es doger","dish/es-doger"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Geplak","dish/geplak"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/7/73/Sate_Bandeng_2.jpg/500px-Sate_Bandeng_2.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Gunawan Kartapranata","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sate_Bandeng_2.jpg"}},"ingredient/provolone":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/provolone","n":"Provolone","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Provolone is an Italian semi-hard cheese made from cow's milk. It is an aged pasta filata ('stretched-curd') cheese originating in the Campania region, near Vesuvius, where it is still produced in pear, sausage, or cone shapes 10 to 15 cm (4 to 6 in) long. Provolone-type cheeses are also produced in other countries. The most important provolone production region today is northwestern Italy and, in particular, the city of Cremona. Provolone, provola, and provoleta are versions of the same basic cheese. Some versions of provolone are smoked."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-provolone"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Provolone"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Italian cuisine","cuisine/italian-cuisine",[["Spaghetti alla Nerano","dish/spaghetti-alla-nerano"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Basil","ingredient/basil"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Extra virgin olive oil","ingredient/extra-virgin-olive-oil"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Parmesan","ingredient/parmesan"],["Spaghetti","ingredient/spaghetti"],["Zucchini","ingredient/zucchini"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a0/Provolone_watermelon_shape.jpg/500px-Provolone_watermelon_shape.jpg","w":500,"h":667,"by":"like the grand canyon","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provolone_watermelon_shape.jpg"}},"dish/sufganiyah":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/sufganiyah","n":"Sufganiyah","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Israeli cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Sufganiyah is a round, jelly doughnut–like pastry, eaten around the world during the Jewish festival of Hanukkah. The doughnut is deep-fried, injected with jam or custard, and then topped with powdered sugar. The doughnut recipe originated in Europe in the 16th century, and by the 19th century was known as a Berliner in Germany and a Religieuse in France. Polish Jews, who called them ponchki, fried the doughnuts in schmaltz rather than lard due to kashrut laws. The ponchik was brought to Israel by Polish Jewish immigrants, where it was renamed the sufganiyah based on the Talmud's description of a \"spongy dough\"."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-sufganiyah"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sufganiyah"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Israeli cuisine","cuisine/israeli-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Israeli cuisine","links":[["Baba ghanoush","dish/baba-ghanoush"],["Bavel (restaurant)","dish/bavel-restaurant"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Hummus","dish/hummus"],["Lahoh","d